COPY gold.ssot_market_news (id, headline, lede, body_md, asof, category, related_instruments, image_url, source, source_url, agent_run_id, retrieved_at) FROM stdin;
33137411-ec62-4041-bc25-3760d0f82781	Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement	The Federal Reserve released its FOMC statement on October 28, 2026, at the conclusion of the committee's policy meeting.	The Federal Reserve issued its Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) statement on October 28, 2026. The release item does not include the policy decision, the target range for the federal funds rate, or any forward guidance, so the outcome cannot be confirmed from this item alone.\n\nFOMC statements are a primary driver of US rate expectations. The rate decision and the tone of the language on inflation, employment and balance-sheet policy could shift short-dated Treasury yields most directly, with spillovers to longer maturities. A more hawkish or dovish message than markets expect would likely move the dollar, US equity indices and rate-sensitive emerging-market currencies.\n\nThe key details to check in the full statement are the target range, any dissents and changes in the economic assessment. These will set the direction of the market reaction.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20261028a.htm)	2026-10-28 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY,SPX,NDX,DJI,EURUSD,USDINR}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20261028a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:00:01.382993+00
6753301c-b719-41f8-93cc-b51eb8cd301d	Fed terminates enforcement actions with Gardner Bancshares and Small Business Bank	The Federal Reserve Board announced the termination of enforcement actions with Gardner Bancshares, Inc. and Small Business Bank.	The Federal Reserve Board announced it has terminated enforcement actions with Gardner Bancshares, Inc. and Small Business Bank. The release does not give further details on the original actions or the reasons for ending them.\n\nThe economic impact is likely negligible. This is a routine supervisory step involving a small institution. Ending an enforcement action generally signals that the regulator considers the identified issues resolved. That could modestly ease operating constraints for the bank involved. It is unlikely to affect monetary policy, interest rates, FX or broader markets.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20261016a.htm)	2026-10-16 15:00:00+00	rates	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20261016a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:00:05.818741+00
da2744bf-5559-442a-974f-9341ca4552d5	Fed releases minutes of discount rate meetings from August 17, September 9, and September 16, 2026	The Federal Reserve Board published minutes of its discount rate meetings held on August 17, September 9, and September 16, 2026.	The Federal Reserve Board released minutes of its discount rate meetings held on August 17, September 9, and September 16, 2026. The release provides a record of Board deliberations on the primary credit (discount) rate during that period. The item does not state the decisions or rates discussed.\n\nDiscount rate minutes typically track decisions already reflected in FOMC policy announcements, so market impact is likely limited. However, the minutes could offer additional color on how individual Reserve Banks viewed economic conditions and the policy outlook. Any signals on the rate path could marginally affect front-end Treasury yields and the dollar.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20261013a.htm)	2026-10-13 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20261013a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:00:21.910239+00
06f546ee-096e-4c0f-b0da-7dba463bd4a2	Fed Board releases results of 2025 Survey of Consumer Finances on economic condition of American families	The Federal Reserve Board released results of its 2025 Survey of Consumer Finances, offering detailed insights into the economic condition of American families.	The Federal Reserve Board has released the results of the 2025 Survey of Consumer Finances. The survey provides the public and policymakers with detailed insights into the economic condition of American families.\n\nThe release is a periodic data publication rather than a policy action, so any direct market impact is likely limited. Its findings on household finances could inform policymakers' and analysts' views of consumer resilience, which may feed indirectly into expectations for consumer spending and the rates outlook over time.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/other20261009a.htm)	2026-10-09 14:00:00+00	economy	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/other20261009a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:00:32.31859+00
efa34261-b383-4474-b8b0-881f5794e046	Fed announces enforcement action against American Express over failures to detect and report suspicious money-laundering activity	The Federal Reserve Board announced an enforcement action against American Express Company to address, among other things, its failure to sufficiently detect and report certain suspicious activity related to money laundering.	The Federal Reserve Board announced an enforcement action against American Express Company. The release states that the action addresses, among other things, the firm's failure to sufficiently detect and report certain suspicious activity related to money laundering. The announcement gives no further detail on penalties or remediation terms.\n\nThe macroeconomic impact is likely minimal. This is a firm-specific supervisory matter and does not signal a change in monetary policy, rates or broader financial conditions. Any market effect would likely be confined to American Express shares and possibly sentiment toward card issuers and financial-sector compliance costs. The direct effect on broad equity indices is likely negligible.\n\nMore broadly, the action reflects continued regulatory focus on anti-money-laundering controls at large financial firms. That focus could modestly raise compliance spending across the sector over time.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20261008a.htm)	2026-10-08 20:30:00+00	markets	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20261008a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:00:38.131002+00
f61f442c-ff53-4854-8f76-20829543d67b	Fed terminates enforcement action with SNB Bancshares and Bank of Eufaula	The Federal Reserve Board announced the termination of an enforcement action with SNB Bancshares and Bank of Eufaula.	The Federal Reserve Board announced it has terminated an enforcement action with SNB Bancshares and Bank of Eufaula. The release does not give further details on the action or why it was terminated.\n\nThe economic impact is likely negligible. This is a routine supervisory matter involving a small institution, and it is unlikely to affect prices, interest rates, FX, trade or fiscal conditions. Ending an enforcement action generally indicates the bank's supervisory issues have been resolved, which could slightly ease its operating constraints. It carries no signal for monetary policy.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20260918b.htm)	2026-09-18 15:00:00+00	economy	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20260918b.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:02:02.761126+00
90ca1ae5-6ee0-4ed1-aab0-d917d409a5f3	Fed releases minutes of September 15-16, 2026 FOMC meeting	The Federal Reserve published the minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee's September 15-16, 2026 policy meeting.	The Federal Reserve released the minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee meeting held September 15-16, 2026. The release itself does not detail the committee's discussion or its policy outlook.\n\nMarkets typically read FOMC minutes for signals on the future path of the policy rate and the balance of views among participants. A more hawkish tone than expected could lift short-dated Treasury yields and the dollar and weigh on equities. A more dovish tone could do the opposite. Shifts in rate expectations could also spill over into emerging-market currencies such as the rupee and yuan, as well as into dollar-pegged Gulf markets.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20261007a.htm)	2026-10-07 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY,EURUSD,SPX,NDX}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20261007a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:00:49.449503+00
b4477a43-e866-4e10-ba4b-5ccef0793d5d	Federal Reserve Board approves application by Isabella Bank Corporation	The Federal Reserve Board announced it has approved an application by Isabella Bank Corporation.	The Federal Reserve Board announced approval of an application submitted by Isabella Bank Corporation. The release, issued as a Board order, does not specify the nature of the application in the information provided.\n\nThis appears to be a routine regulatory action affecting a single banking organization rather than a monetary policy decision. It is unlikely to have a meaningful effect on interest rates, inflation, FX, or broader market pricing. Any impact would likely be confined to the institution involved and its local banking market.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/orders20261005a.htm)	2026-10-05 20:30:00+00	economy	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/orders20261005a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:00:53.83452+00
c838e64a-47a7-4ab2-9058-279df10f9749	Federal Reserve Board approves application by Fleur Capital Corporation	The Federal Reserve Board announced it has approved an application by Fleur Capital Corporation.	The Federal Reserve Board announced its approval of an application submitted by Fleur Capital Corporation. The release does not describe what the application covers or give any figures.\n\nThis is a routine regulatory order, not a monetary policy action. It is unlikely to affect interest rates, the policy rate path, currencies or broader markets. Any economic effect would probably stay with the firm involved and its local banking market.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/orders20261002a.htm)	2026-10-02 20:45:00+00	economy	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/orders20261002a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:00:57.323681+00
35b9d4c1-cb59-4ccb-adbe-27771ecd8aa5	Fed extends comment period on Regulation O modernization proposal to November 4	The Federal Reserve Board said it will extend, until November 4, the public comment period on its proposal to modernize Regulation O.	The Federal Reserve Board announced that it will extend the comment period on its proposal to modernize Regulation O. Comments will now be accepted until November 4. The release gives no reason for the extension and no details of the proposal's substance.\n\nThe item is procedural and bank-supervisory rather than a monetary policy action, so any direct market impact is likely negligible. It has no bearing on the policy rate or Treasury yields. A longer comment window could modestly delay finalization of the rule. Any eventual effects would likely be confined to bank compliance and governance practices rather than broader prices, rates, FX or trade channels.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20261002a.htm)	2026-10-02 20:00:00+00	economy	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20261002a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:01:03.511653+00
71a62bfd-521f-4891-a124-5b9035e6dd08	Federal Reserve Board issues enforcement action with Ontario Bancorporation, Inc.	The Federal Reserve Board announced an enforcement action with Ontario Bancorporation, Inc.; the release gives no further detail on its terms.	The Federal Reserve Board issued an enforcement action with Ontario Bancorporation, Inc. The announcement does not describe the nature, terms or cause of the action.\n\nThe broader economic impact is likely negligible. Enforcement actions against individual banking organizations are routine supervisory steps. Absent signs of wider stress, an action like this is unlikely to move rates, FX or equity indices. It could matter locally for the institution's lending and operations, depending on its terms.\n\nThe item is mainly a marker of ongoing bank supervision rather than a signal on monetary policy.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20261002a.htm)	2026-10-02 15:00:00+00	economy	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20261002a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:01:10.153604+00
c6ab4a92-d252-4408-9506-3b6e7140fa7c	Fed finalizes stress test changes to boost transparency and reduce volatility in related capital requirements	The Federal Reserve Board finalized changes intended to make its stress test more transparent and publicly accountable and to reduce volatility in stress test-related capital requirements.	The Federal Reserve Board has finalized changes to its bank stress test framework. It says the changes are aimed at enhancing the transparency and public accountability of the test and at reducing volatility in the capital requirements tied to stress test results. The release does not include specific figures.\n\nThe likely economic impact runs mainly through bank capital and credit. Less volatile capital requirements could give large banks more predictable capital planning, which might support lending capacity and shareholder distributions at the margin. Greater transparency could reduce uncertainty for investors in bank equities. Any effect on broader rates or FX is likely to be limited, because this is a supervisory measure rather than a monetary policy decision. Bank-heavy equity indices could see modest sentiment effects.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20260930a.htm)	2026-09-30 13:00:00+00	rates	{SPX,DJI}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20260930a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:01:15.703604+00
