3b7a594d-75e8-4bba-8717-ca466d890505	Agencies publish resolution plan feedback letters for 15 banking organizations	US banking agencies published feedback letters on the resolution plans submitted by 15 banking organizations, according to a Federal Reserve release.	US banking agencies, in a release posted by the Federal Reserve, published feedback letters on the resolution plans of 15 banking organizations. Resolution plans, often called "living wills," describe how a bank could be wound down in an orderly way under bankruptcy if it failed. The release text available here does not name the institutions or say whether any plans were found deficient.\n\nThe announcement is a routine supervisory step and is unlikely to move broad markets on its own. Any effect would most likely come through bank equities and funding costs. If the letters ask for remediation, the affected banks could face higher compliance or capital costs. Letters that raise no major concerns could modestly support confidence in large banks.\n\nThere is no direct read-through to policy rates, FX or commodities from this item.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20260929a.htm)	2026-09-29 20:00:00+00	economy	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20260929a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:01:20.60258+00
a975cd3b-fc49-4dc1-906b-97ad55904530	Federal Reserve Board approves application by Peoples Bancorp Inc.	The Federal Reserve Board announced it has approved an application submitted by Peoples Bancorp Inc.	The Federal Reserve Board announced its approval of an application by Peoples Bancorp Inc. The release does not describe the nature of the application, the parties involved beyond the applicant, or any financial terms.\n\nThis is a routine bank regulatory action, not a monetary policy decision. It is unlikely to have a meaningful effect on interest rates, inflation, FX, or broad equity indices. Any impact would likely be confined to the applicant and possibly regional banking peers. Such approvals can be read as a modest signal of how the regulator views bank applications, but this single order carries little macro weight.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/orders20260925a.htm)	2026-09-25 20:30:00+00	markets	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/orders20260925a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:01:26.272148+00
214f7b9a-cbff-4a83-a3ef-cb2288a1629b	Federal Reserve Board requests public comment on two proposals related to establishing a regulatory framework for Board-supervised payment stablecoin issuers under the GENIUS Act	Federal Reserve Board requests public comment on two proposals related to establishing a regulatory framework for Board-supervised payment stablecoin issuers under the GENIUS Act	Federal Reserve Board requests public comment on two proposals related to establishing a regulatory framework for Board-supervised payment stablecoin issuers under the GENIUS Act\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20260924a.htm)	2026-09-24 18:30:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST10}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20260924a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:01:39.546271+00
801e2e57-4e85-4d2f-808e-be74fb9ec96b	Federal Reserve Board issues enforcement action with former employee of Sandy Spring Bank	The Federal Reserve Board announced an enforcement action involving a former employee of Sandy Spring Bank.	The Federal Reserve Board issued an enforcement action involving a former employee of Sandy Spring Bank. The release does not give the nature of the action or the underlying conduct.\n\nThis is a routine supervisory matter aimed at one individual, not at the institution or the wider banking sector. It is unlikely to have any measurable effect on prices, interest rates, FX, trade or fiscal conditions. It carries no signal about monetary policy.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20260924a.htm)	2026-09-24 15:00:00+00	economy	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20260924a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:01:43.70114+00
811b3efe-1f99-43d9-aa18-563d39496511	Federal Reserve Board approves application by BancFirst Corporation	The Federal Reserve Board announced its approval of an application by BancFirst Corporation.	The Federal Reserve Board announced that it has approved an application by BancFirst Corporation. The release does not describe the nature of the application.\n\nThis is a routine regulatory order concerning a single bank holding company. It is unlikely to have a meaningful effect on prices, interest rates, FX, trade or fiscal conditions. Any impact would likely be confined to the institution involved and its immediate local markets. The action does not signal a change in monetary policy.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/orders20260922a.htm)	2026-09-22 20:30:00+00	economy	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/orders20260922a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:01:47.476475+00
e80a29da-e1ee-4243-afcb-161712b12397	Fed issues enforcement actions against former employees of Northstar Bank, American Express Travel Related Services, and Regions Bank	The Federal Reserve Board announced enforcement actions involving individual former employees of Northstar Bank, American Express Travel Related Services Company, Inc., and Regions Bank.	The Federal Reserve Board issued enforcement actions with three individuals: a former employee of Northstar Bank, a former employee of American Express Travel Related Services Company, Inc., and a former employee of Regions Bank. The release does not specify the nature of the conduct or the terms of the actions.\n\nBecause the actions target former employees rather than the institutions themselves, the macroeconomic impact is likely negligible. There is no evident channel to prices, interest rates, FX, trade, or fiscal policy. The release is routine supervisory housekeeping and is unlikely to move broad market indices or the policy outlook.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20260918a.htm)	2026-09-18 15:00:00+00	economy	{}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/enforcement20260918a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:01:57.625905+00
37fb8bf7-840b-4f21-b93e-9a8e84c7dfeb	LNG imports make up increasing share of Colombia's natural gas supply	Colombia is importing more liquefied natural gas (LNG) as domestic production continues to fall. A potential drought this fall and winter could limit the availability of hydroelectric generation and boost the country's demand for natural gas power generation. Colombia's governmen	Colombia is importing more liquefied natural gas (LNG) as domestic production continues to fall. A potential drought this fall and winter could limit the availability of hydroelectric generation and boost the country's demand for natural gas power generation. Colombia's government has declared a national disaster in anticipation of a drought resulting from an expected strong El Niño.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68364)	2026-10-29 14:00:00+00	markets	{}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68364	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747839+00
0524ccdc-c936-44d7-bac4-a13a10ce7ca6	Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement	The Federal Reserve released its FOMC monetary policy statement on September 16, 2026; the item does not say what the committee decided.	The Federal Reserve published its Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) statement on September 16, 2026. The release item does not include the policy decision, the target range for the federal funds rate, or the committee's guidance.\n\nFOMC statements are a primary driver of US rate expectations. Depending on the decision and the tone of the guidance, short-dated Treasury yields (UST2) are likely to react most directly, with spillover to longer yields (UST10). The dollar (DXY) and major pairs such as EURUSD, GBPUSD, USDCNY and USDINR could move as rate differentials shift. US equity indices could also reprice as discount-rate expectations change.\n\nGulf currencies are pegged to the dollar, so Gulf markets often track Fed policy closely. Any change in US rates could feed through to regional financing conditions and equity benchmarks.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260916a.htm)	2026-09-16 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY,EURUSD,GBPUSD,USDCNY,USDINR,SPX,NDX,DJI}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260916a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:02:14.648726+00
f634c675-fd4a-4d52-acb7-abd8a71348a6	Fed releases economic projections from September 15-16 FOMC meeting	The Federal Reserve Board and the Federal Open Market Committee released economic projections from their September 15-16, 2026 FOMC meeting.	The Federal Reserve Board and the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) released economic projections from the September 15-16, 2026 meeting. The release itself does not include specific figures. Such projections typically cover policymakers' expectations for growth, unemployment, inflation and the path of the policy rate.\n\nThe projections could shape market expectations for the future path of the federal funds rate. Shifts in the implied rate path would likely transmit first to short-dated Treasury yields, which are most sensitive to policy guidance. Longer-dated yields could also move. Changes in US rate expectations could affect the dollar against major and emerging-market currencies. Equity indices may react via discount-rate and growth-outlook channels.\n\nThe direction of any market move depends on how the projections compare with prior expectations. The release does not provide that detail, so no directional call is made here.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - All Releases](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260916b.htm)	2026-09-16 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY,EURUSD,SPX,NDX}	\N	FRB: Press Release - All Releases	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260916b.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:02:19.9505+00
82960f54-fa76-4e03-8c0f-6dbcaac7a9c5	Fed releases minutes of Board discount rate meetings on July 20 and July 29, 2026	The Federal Reserve Board published minutes of its discount rate meetings held on July 20 and July 29, 2026.	The Federal Reserve Board released the minutes of its discount rate meetings held on July 20 and July 29, 2026. The release itself does not summarize the decisions or deliberations, and no figures were provided in the item.\n\nDiscount rate minutes typically show requests from regional Reserve Bank boards to maintain or change the primary credit rate. They can offer some insight into how district banks view economic conditions and the policy outlook. Any market impact would likely be limited, since these minutes usually trail FOMC decisions. Still, signs of divergent views among Reserve Banks could marginally affect rate expectations, Treasury yields and the dollar.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260825a.htm)	2026-08-25 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY}	\N	FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260825a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:02:31.455171+00
6fe2fac7-73a1-44c4-bf4b-deeea03e4f3c	Fed releases minutes of July 28–29, 2026 FOMC meeting	The Federal Reserve published the minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee meeting held July 28–29, 2026.	The Federal Reserve released the minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee's July 28–29, 2026 meeting on August 19, 2026. The release itself does not summarize the discussion, and no policy details are given in the item.\n\nFOMC minutes are closely read for signals on the committee's views of inflation, growth and the future path of the policy rate. Depending on their tone, they could shift rate expectations. That would likely move short-dated Treasury yields most directly, with possible spillovers to longer yields, the US dollar and equity indices. A more hawkish reading than markets expect could lift yields and the dollar and weigh on stocks. A more dovish reading could do the opposite.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260819a.htm)	2026-08-19 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY,SPX,NDX}	\N	FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260819a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:02:43.095273+00
2d7d5875-ae61-4bbc-afdb-7bf8c449217f	Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement	The Federal Reserve released its FOMC monetary policy statement on July 29, 2026; the item does not say what the Committee decided.	The Federal Reserve published a Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) statement on July 29, 2026. The release does not report the policy decision, the target range for the federal funds rate, or any change in guidance.\n\nFOMC statements are a main driver of US rate expectations. Depending on the decision and the tone of the guidance, the statement could move short-dated Treasury yields most directly, along with longer yields. It could also move the dollar and US equity indices. A more hawkish-than-expected outcome would likely lift yields and the dollar and weigh on equities. A dovish surprise would likely do the reverse. Spillovers to emerging-market currencies such as the rupee and yuan are also possible through dollar moves.\n\nMarket impact cannot be assessed until the statement's content is known. Readers should check the full text for the rate decision and forward guidance.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260729a.htm)	2026-07-29 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY,SPX,NDX,DJI,EURUSD,USDINR,USDCNY}	\N	FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260729a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:02:56.453822+00
15dda54a-ac84-44d1-ad85-ac4436a4099e	U.S. natural gas production reached a record high in July 2026	U.S. natural gas production hit an all-time high in July 2026, driven primarily by increased output in the Permian Basin, according to our latest Natural Gas Monthly.	U.S. natural gas production hit an all-time high in July 2026, driven primarily by increased output in the Permian Basin, according to our latest Natural Gas Monthly.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68225)	2026-10-02 14:00:00+00	markets	{}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68225	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747905+00
1f43354a-5238-4e63-8e33-c77614be1268	Fed releases minutes of Board's discount rate meetings on June 8 and June 17, 2026	The Federal Reserve Board published minutes of its discount rate meetings held on June 8 and June 17, 2026.	The Federal Reserve Board released minutes of its discount rate meetings held on June 8 and June 17, 2026. These minutes typically record the Board's review of requests from the regional Reserve Banks to set the primary credit (discount) rate. They can also note directors' views on economic conditions in their districts.\n\nThe release is routine, and its market impact is likely limited. Any commentary on regional economic conditions or on the stance of policy could offer marginal insight into the Fed's thinking on rates. That could modestly influence Treasury yields, particularly at the short end, and the dollar if it diverges from expectations. The item itself provides no specific details or figures.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260714a.htm)	2026-07-14 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY}	\N	FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260714a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:03:08.449167+00
84b1e827-4b50-444f-b90f-9b9f2fa368cd	Fed names leadership and objectives of task forces to advance conduct of monetary policy	The Federal Reserve announced the leadership and objectives of its task forces aimed at advancing the conduct of monetary policy.	The Federal Reserve announced the leadership and objectives of task forces set up to advance how it conducts monetary policy. The release provided no further detail on specific policy changes, rate decisions or timelines.\n\nThe announcement is institutional and procedural, not a policy action, so near-term market impact is likely limited. Over time, the task forces' work could shape the Fed's framework, communications or implementation tools. That could influence how markets read future rate guidance, with possible knock-on effects for Treasury yields and the dollar. Investors may watch for any later findings that signal changes to how policy is set or communicated.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260709a.htm)	2026-07-09 19:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10}	\N	FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260709a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:03:13.443696+00
1730baf8-63d3-41d1-8616-1ccffd2c89cd	Fed releases minutes of June 16-17, 2026 FOMC meeting	The Federal Reserve published the minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee's June 16-17, 2026 policy meeting.	The Federal Reserve Board released the minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee meeting held June 16-17, 2026. The release item does not summarize the discussion, so it gives no details on policymakers' views, votes or rate guidance.\n\nFOMC minutes are closely read for the balance of views on inflation, growth and the path of the policy rate. Depending on their tone, they could shift expectations for the federal funds rate. That would likely show up first in short-dated Treasury yields (UST2), then in the 10-year yield and the dollar (DXY, EURUSD). A more hawkish read would typically lift yields and the dollar and weigh on equities. A dovish read could do the opposite. Any market reaction depends on how the content compares with what investors already expect.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260708a.htm)	2026-07-08 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY,EURUSD,SPX,NDX}	\N	FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260708a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:03:19.433344+00
6ec3dc21-0583-410e-b523-f8b91ce9bed2	Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement	Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement	Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260617a.htm)	2026-06-17 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST10}	\N	FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260617a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:03:31.502626+00
1e77afca-5d9b-4ecb-aa1e-ad9134067ccb	Fed releases economic projections from June 16-17 FOMC meeting	The Federal Reserve Board and the Federal Open Market Committee released economic projections from their June 16-17 FOMC meeting.	The Federal Reserve Board and the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) released economic projections from the June 16-17 FOMC meeting. The release does not include specific figures in the information provided.\n\nFOMC projections typically cover participants' views on growth, unemployment, inflation and the appropriate path of the policy rate. Markets will likely compare them with prior expectations. A more hawkish-than-expected policy path could push short-dated Treasury yields and the dollar higher and weigh on equities. A more dovish outlook could have the opposite effect. Shifts in the expected US rate path could also feed through to emerging-market currencies such as the rupee and yuan via dollar strength or weakness.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260617b.htm)	2026-06-17 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY,SPX,NDX,EURUSD,USDINR,USDCNY}	\N	FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260617b.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:03:43.261155+00
11093fd2-0727-4199-a8c9-efea4ebeef97	Fed releases minutes of Board's discount rate meetings on April 20 and 29, 2026	The Federal Reserve Board published minutes of its discount rate meetings held on April 20 and 29, 2026.	The Federal Reserve Board released the minutes of its discount rate meetings held on April 20 and April 29, 2026. The item supplied gives no detail on the decisions or deliberations recorded in the minutes.\n\nDiscount rate minutes typically show which Reserve Bank boards asked for changes to the primary credit rate. They can offer a modest read on regional sentiment toward the policy stance. Any signal from these minutes would most likely reach the market through short-term rate expectations, front-end Treasury yields and the dollar. The effect is usually limited because the minutes cover meetings already overtaken by subsequent FOMC communications.\n\nWithout specifics on the content, the market impact is likely to be small. Traders may scan the minutes for any divergence among Reserve Banks on the appropriate rate path.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260526a.htm)	2026-05-26 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY}	\N	FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260526a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:03:56.489328+00
