25e726a8-a918-45de-a213-1686ab82617d	Fed releases minutes of April 28-29, 2026 FOMC meeting	The Federal Reserve on May 20, 2026 published the minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee's April 28-29, 2026 policy meeting.	The Federal Reserve released the minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee meeting held April 28-29, 2026. The release itself does not summarize the Committee's discussion or policy outlook.\n\nFOMC minutes are typically read for detail on policymakers' views on inflation, growth, labor markets and the likely path of the policy rate. Depending on whether the tone is read as hawkish or dovish, the minutes could move short-dated Treasury yields most directly, along with longer yields, the dollar and US equity indices. A hawkish read would likely lift yields and the dollar and weigh on stocks. A dovish read could do the reverse. Spillovers to emerging-market currencies and dollar-pegged Gulf markets are possible through the dollar and rate channels.\n\nSource: [FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260520a.htm)	2026-05-20 18:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST2,UST10,DXY,EURUSD,SPX,NDX,DJI}	\N	FRB: Press Release - Monetary Policy	https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260520a.htm	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:04:02.910028+00
229c5a58-77ac-4fe3-9678-105dc820bc45	ECB's Cipollone on next-day securities settlement, tokenisation and Europe's financial infrastructure	ECB Executive Board member Piero Cipollone gave a speech on next-day securities settlement, tokenisation and the transformation of Europe's financial infrastructure.	ECB Executive Board member Piero Cipollone delivered a speech on next-day securities settlement, tokenisation and the transformation of Europe's financial infrastructure. The item includes only the title, with no further detail on the speech's content.\n\nThe topic concerns market plumbing rather than monetary policy, so any direct effect on rates or FX is likely limited. Over time, faster settlement cycles and tokenised assets could lower post-trade costs and counterparty risk in European capital markets. They could also deepen euro-area market integration, which would modestly support the euro's international role.\n\nNo policy signals or figures are reported, so near-term market impact is likely minimal.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261029~e9b57c3495.en.html)	2026-10-29 15:50:00+00	markets	{EURUSD}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261029~e9b57c3495.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:04:14.539008+00
5fc6a226-81db-412b-ade6-affe387d7b82	ECB's Lagarde and Vujčić deliver monetary policy statement	ECB President Christine Lagarde and Boris Vujčić presented the European Central Bank's monetary policy statement on 29 October 2026.	ECB President Christine Lagarde and Boris Vujčić delivered the European Central Bank's monetary policy statement at a press conference on 29 October 2026. The item gives no details on the rate decision or the policy guidance.\n\nThe statement sets the ECB's stance on interest rates and its view of euro-area inflation and growth. Its tone could move euro-area rate expectations and the euro. A more hawkish-than-expected message would likely support EURUSD and weigh on the dollar index. A dovish tilt could do the opposite. Spillovers to global bond yields and equity sentiment are possible but likely secondary.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/press_conference/monetary-policy-statement/2026/html/ecb.is261029~5c5ffe99e5.en.html)	2026-10-29 14:00:00+00	rates	{EURUSD,DXY}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/press_conference/monetary-policy-statement/2026/html/ecb.is261029~5c5ffe99e5.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:04:26.886939+00
