98277012-16e1-49e5-b513-41f46f972380	ECB President Lagarde to step down on 7 February 2027	The European Central Bank announced that President Christine Lagarde will step down on 7 February 2027.	The European Central Bank (ECB) said in a press release that President Christine Lagarde will step down on 7 February 2027. The release headline gives no reasons for the departure and does not name a successor.\n\nThe main economic channel is likely expectations about future euro-area monetary policy. Markets could start pricing the policy leanings of possible successors, which may add uncertainty to the euro-area rate outlook and to the euro. EURUSD could react as investors weigh whether the next president is seen as more hawkish or more dovish. Moves in the euro could in turn affect DXY.\n\nNear-term policy is unlikely to change directly because of the announcement, since the departure date is several months away. The succession process could become a focus for rates and FX markets until a nominee is known.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/pr/date/2026/html/ecb.pr261029~abbb6dc64b.en.html)	2026-10-29 10:00:00+00	rates	{EURUSD,DXY}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/pr/date/2026/html/ecb.pr261029~abbb6dc64b.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:04:46.78756+00
1c96519e-098b-47da-bf8c-a081c6273674	ECB publishes October 2026 euro area bank lending survey	The European Central Bank released its October 2026 euro area bank lending survey, a quarterly gauge of credit standards and loan demand across the bloc.	The European Central Bank (ECB) published its October 2026 euro area bank lending survey. The release provided no detailed findings in the text received, so no figures are reported here.\n\nThe survey typically tracks how banks are changing credit standards and terms for firms and households, and how loan demand is evolving. It is a key input for assessing how ECB policy is passing through to the real economy.\n\nThe economic impact depends on the findings. Signs of tighter credit or weaker loan demand could point to softer euro area growth and add to expectations of more accommodative ECB policy, which would likely weigh on the euro. Easing standards or firmer demand could support growth expectations and the currency. Markets may adjust euro rate expectations and EUR/USD once the details are digested.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/pr/date/2026/html/ecb.pr261027~506ef03145.en.html)	2026-10-27 00:00:00+00	economy	{EURUSD}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/pr/date/2026/html/ecb.pr261027~506ef03145.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:04:59.425203+00
