843b245d-c205-43d9-929a-702882b3ac46	ECB's Philip R. Lane speaks on euro liquidity lines and the international monetary system	ECB Executive Board member Philip R. Lane delivered a speech on euro liquidity lines and their role in the international monetary system.	ECB Executive Board member Philip R. Lane gave a speech titled "Euro liquidity lines and the international monetary system." The source provides no further details on the speech's content, specific proposals or policy signals.\n\nEuro liquidity lines, such as swap and repo arrangements with other central banks, are tools that can support euro funding conditions abroad during periods of market stress. Commentary on expanding or formalising these facilities could, over time, bear on the euro's international role and on cross-border funding markets. Any direct effect on EURUSD or euro-area rates is likely to be limited unless the speech signals concrete policy changes, which the available text does not indicate.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261021~401d6c4b70.en.html)	2026-10-21 04:15:00+00	rates	{EURUSD}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261021~401d6c4b70.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:06:03.920813+00
972fd364-1283-483b-89cc-a980dfc43fc0	Frank Elderson: Interview with Die Presse	ECB Executive Board member Frank Elderson gave an interview to Austrian newspaper Die Presse, published by the ECB on 20 October 2026.	The European Central Bank published an interview with Frank Elderson, a member of its Executive Board, conducted by the Austrian newspaper Die Presse. The item as provided includes no details of the topics discussed or of any policy views expressed.\n\nCommentary from ECB Executive Board members can shape market expectations for euro-area monetary policy. Depending on its content, the interview could influence euro-area rate expectations and, through them, the euro's exchange rate. Without details of what was said, the direction and size of any impact cannot be assessed and is likely limited.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/inter/date/2026/html/ecb.in261020~97e2e4bc38.en.html)	2026-10-20 07:00:00+00	rates	{EURUSD}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/inter/date/2026/html/ecb.in261020~97e2e4bc38.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:06:14.43655+00
a5644f49-ca04-4ba3-9275-27aad1b2077d	ECB's Frank Elderson: From climate and nature risks to economic resilience and competitiveness	ECB official Frank Elderson delivered a speech linking climate and nature-related risks to economic resilience and competitiveness.	European Central Bank official Frank Elderson gave a speech titled "From climate and nature risks to economic resilience and competitiveness." The item provides only the title, so the specific arguments, data or policy proposals are not available here.\n\nJudging by the title, the speech frames climate and nature risks as factors that bear on the euro area's economic resilience and competitiveness, not only on environmental outcomes. Remarks of this kind are usually relevant to supervisory expectations for banks and to longer-term structural themes, rather than to near-term interest-rate decisions.\n\nAny direct market impact is likely limited. If the speech signals tighter supervisory scrutiny of climate and nature risks, it could influence bank risk management and green financing over time. Effects on rates or the euro would likely be marginal.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261019~f0a3e053de.en.html)	2026-10-19 06:00:00+00	economy	{}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261019~f0a3e053de.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:06:26.294145+00
651205a3-31e2-4e7d-8ee2-d5f144d3d30d	Christine Lagarde: IMFC Statement	ECB President Christine Lagarde has issued a statement to the International Monetary and Financial Committee (IMFC), published by the European Central Bank.	ECB President Christine Lagarde delivered a statement to the International Monetary and Financial Committee (IMFC), the IMF's policy advisory body. The ECB published it on 17 October 2026. The item gives no further detail on the statement's contents.\n\nIMFC statements by the ECB President usually summarise the euro area's economic outlook and the ECB's monetary policy stance for an international audience. Without the text, no specific policy signal can be identified. Any change in tone on inflation, growth or policy direction could shift expectations for ECB rates, and with them the euro exchange rate. If the statement repeats existing guidance, market impact will likely be limited.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261017~7015ea3099.en.html)	2026-10-17 05:00:00+00	rates	{EURUSD}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261017~7015ea3099.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:06:37.676229+00
082ffdb6-e6a2-419a-bb7f-52c5b2e9919d	Frank Elderson: Ensuring resilience in the face of uncertainty	ECB official Frank Elderson delivered a speech on ensuring resilience in the face of uncertainty, according to the European Central Bank.	Frank Elderson of the European Central Bank delivered a speech titled "Ensuring resilience in the face of uncertainty." The item provides only the title. It gives no details on the specific remarks, policy signals or figures discussed.\n\nThe title points to a resilience theme. This could signal emphasis on financial-sector or supervisory preparedness rather than near-term monetary policy guidance. Any direct impact on euro-area rates or the euro is therefore likely limited. Markets could still read such commentary for hints on the ECB's view of risks to the outlook. Signals on risk tolerance or banking-sector buffers could marginally affect EUR sentiment.\n\nSource: [ECB - European Central Bank](https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261014~e4ca33588e.en.html)	2026-10-14 00:00:00+00	economy	{EURUSD}	\N	ECB - European Central Bank	https://www.ecb.europa.eu//press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261014~e4ca33588e.en.html	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:06:48.980447+00
b0269da0-75dc-4f9a-b044-2aebe31cbf9b	EIA expects a mixed picture for winter fuel costs this year	In the 2026-27 Winter Fuels Outlook, released alongside the October Short-Term Energy Outlook (STEO), the U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) expects U.S. households that primarily heat with natural gas or propane - about half of households - will spend less on energy ex	In the 2026-27 Winter Fuels Outlook, released alongside the October Short-Term Energy Outlook (STEO), the U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) expects U.S. households that primarily heat with natural gas or propane - about half of households - will spend less on energy expenses this winter than last winter. By contrast, households that primarily use electricity or heating oil for space heating are expected to spend more.\n\nSource: [EIA: Press Releases](/pressroom/releases/press593.php)	2026-10-06 17:00:00+00	commodities	{BRENT_SPOT}	\N	EIA: Press Releases	/pressroom/releases/press593.php	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:01.602037+00
e7de2466-43bd-4ca2-bd6f-256141ebb1a5	EIA expects record electricity generation in 2026 and 2027	The U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) expects U.S. electricity generation will grow 2.2% to a record 4,368 billion kilowatthours (BkWh) this year. EIA expects an additional 1.7% growth in electricity generation next year.	The U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) expects U.S. electricity generation will grow 2.2% to a record 4,368 billion kilowatthours (BkWh) this year. EIA expects an additional 1.7% growth in electricity generation next year.\n\nSource: [EIA: Press Releases](/pressroom/releases/press592.php)	2026-09-09 17:00:00+00	markets	{}	\N	EIA: Press Releases	/pressroom/releases/press592.php	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:14.779302+00
52f89319-001f-41c5-bb64-85c48e55a7fa	EIA expects highest natural gas inventories in a decade heading into winter	The EIA expects U.S. natural gas inventories to reach 3,985 Bcf in October, the most gas in storage ahead of the winter heating season since 2016.	The U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) expects natural gas inventories to rise to 3,985 billion cubic feet (Bcf) in October. That would be the most gas in storage ahead of the winter heating season since 2016.\n\nAbove-normal storage before winter would likely ease pressure on U.S. natural gas prices and reduce the risk of winter price spikes. Lower heating and power-generation costs could slightly ease household energy bills. That could help moderate headline inflation, though the effect on broader price measures and rate expectations is likely to be small.\n\nAmple domestic supply could also support U.S. LNG export availability. Any spillover into crude oil benchmarks is likely limited, since gas and oil markets are priced largely independently in the U.S.\n\nSource: [EIA: Press Releases](/pressroom/releases/press591.php)	2026-08-11 17:00:00+00	commodities	{}	\N	EIA: Press Releases	/pressroom/releases/press591.php	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:21.230347+00
