f6812b1c-2671-4db5-999c-9757bb2cac4d	U.S. refineries have been running at very high rates in 2026	The U.S. refinery fleet has run at higher-than-normal rates this year, with refinery utilization averaging more than 95% of capacity between May and September, the highest for those months since 1998. Tight global product supplies this year have boosted margins for petroleum prod	The U.S. refinery fleet has run at higher-than-normal rates this year, with refinery utilization averaging more than 95% of capacity between May and September, the highest for those months since 1998. Tight global product supplies this year have boosted margins for petroleum products, particularly for diesel, incentivizing the increased run rates.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68344)	2026-10-22 14:00:00+00	rates	{FED_UPPER,UST10}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68344	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747844+00
9c64b1fe-7703-4c7e-9d1d-d437774f3f76	Hybrid sales rise while battery electric sales remain lower after tax credit expiration	In the third quarter of 2026 (3Q26), 23% of new light-duty vehicles sold in the United States were hybrid electric, battery electric, or plug-in hybrid electric vehicles, down slightly from 24% in 3Q25. Between 3Q25 and 3Q26, hybrid electric vehicles continued to gain market shar	In the third quarter of 2026 (3Q26), 23% of new light-duty vehicles sold in the United States were hybrid electric, battery electric, or plug-in hybrid electric vehicles, down slightly from 24% in 3Q25. Between 3Q25 and 3Q26, hybrid electric vehicles continued to gain market share, reaching 16% of light-duty vehicle sales. Growth of hybrid electric vehicle sales has slowed this quarter as the average transaction price for a new vehicle passed $50,000-the first time this price has been reached so\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68325)	2026-10-19 14:00:00+00	markets	{}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68325	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747854+00
4cc284cd-1207-40e1-9b38-83833a2acd79	U.S. small-scale solar generation has quadrupled in the last decade	We estimate that U.S. generation from small-scale or rooftop solar photovoltaic systems (smaller than 1 megawatt of capacity) has increased from 18,812 gigawatthours (GWh) to 92,979 GWh over the last decade. The four states with the highest generation of small-scale solar in the 	We estimate that U.S. generation from small-scale or rooftop solar photovoltaic systems (smaller than 1 megawatt of capacity) has increased from 18,812 gigawatthours (GWh) to 92,979 GWh over the last decade. The four states with the highest generation of small-scale solar in the country-California, Arizona, Texas, and Florida-are in regions with more abundant solar resources. Even though it is in a region with lower solar resources, New York ranks as the fifth-largest rooftop solar generation pr\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68324)	2026-10-16 14:00:00+00	markets	{}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68324	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747862+00
