cbcca502-f0b5-4ad6-92d5-6e17f6ebb56d	Category 2 Hurricane Isaias nears landfall, leading to production shut-ins	As of 4:00 a.m. central time on October 9, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration expects Hurricane Isaias to make landfall on the Gulf Coast late Friday or early Saturday, creating the potential for significant disruptions to energy infrastructure. Hurricane Isaias 	As of 4:00 a.m. central time on October 9, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration expects Hurricane Isaias to make landfall on the Gulf Coast late Friday or early Saturday, creating the potential for significant disruptions to energy infrastructure. Hurricane Isaias is the first hurricane of the 2026 Atlantic season and will be the largest storm system to make landfall in the region since Hurricane Helene in September 2024.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68284)	2026-10-29 19:59:34.214897+00	markets	{}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68284	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747892+00
86884b91-3464-46e6-9059-9bf00d81dffc	Mixed outlook for energy expenditures this winter	We expect energy expenditures this winter to vary because of diverging trends in energy prices among fuels and regional variation in forecast temperatures. We expect that lower prices for natural gas and propane will drive decreases in expenditures this year for the half of U.S. 	We expect energy expenditures this winter to vary because of diverging trends in energy prices among fuels and regional variation in forecast temperatures. We expect that lower prices for natural gas and propane will drive decreases in expenditures this year for the half of U.S. households heating with those fuels. Higher prices for electricity and heating oil drive increased expenditures for households heating with those fuels in our forecast.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68264)	2026-10-07 14:00:00+00	commodities	{BRENT_SPOT}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68264	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747896+00
f8206e8a-4b08-427e-945e-61911b4c8a2b	Crude oil prices and refinery margins generally increased throughout the third quarter	Petroleum markets in the third quarter of 2026 (3Q26) were characterized by increasing prices for crude oil and petroleum products amid persistent conflict in the Middle East.	Petroleum markets in the third quarter of 2026 (3Q26) were characterized by increasing prices for crude oil and petroleum products amid persistent conflict in the Middle East.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68245)	2026-10-05 14:00:00+00	commodities	{BRENT_SPOT}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68245	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.7479+00
04cf9c25-c4d8-4784-9201-40c5b03b999c	U.S. exports of propane reached records in the first half of 2026	U.S. propane exports increased to 2 million barrels per day (b/d) in the first half of 2026, 11% more than during the same period in 2025. Increased U.S. propane production and changing trade flows, particularly increased exports to India and other countries in South Asia, drove 	U.S. propane exports increased to 2 million barrels per day (b/d) in the first half of 2026, 11% more than during the same period in 2025. Increased U.S. propane production and changing trade flows, particularly increased exports to India and other countries in South Asia, drove propane exports to a record high of 2.1 million (b/d) in April 2026. Since then, total propane exports, including exports to India, have continued at levels well above the five-year average.\n\nSource: [Today in Energy](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68244)	2026-10-01 14:00:00+00	markets	{}	\N	Today in Energy	https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=68244	33bacda3-c4d5-47b8-8ec4-e885bbd5157f	2026-10-29 20:07:58.747914+00
