cc2607ba-0ff1-4bb3-bfb2-344d81329220	sig_mn_2da818f1c0	promote	A Chinese wealth shock plausibly erodes Dubai tourism-fee and real-estate-fee revenue beyond the AED 0.1bn threshold, though confidence is low given headline-only detail.	-0.15	A large equity wealth shock in China could dampen two Dubai revenue lines. First, Chinese outbound travel and spending (TOUR), which feeds the municipality hotel fee and Tourism Dirham. Second, Chinese buyer activity in off-plan and prime property (RETXN), which feeds the 4% DLD transfer fee. Weaker Chinese risk appetite may also soften re-export demand via Jebel Ali, a PMI and customs channel. The input provides only a headline, so magnitude and persistence are uncertain. The estimate assumes a mid-single-digit decline in Chinese-linked tourism and property flows through 2027.	- Add nationality-level monitoring of DLD transactions and DET arrivals to the monthly fiscal dashboard, with a trigger review if Chinese volumes fall more than 10% year on year.\n- Stress-test the 2027 budget's real-estate fee and tourism fee lines at -5%, and hold related discretionary capex until Q1 2027 data are in.\n- Ask DET to reweight 2027 promotional spend towards India, the GCC and Europe to offset softer Chinese demand.\n- Coordinate with Dubai Customs to watch China-linked re-export volumes as an early PMI and fees signal.	- Guardian report on Chinese market losses: https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/27/chinese-markets-lose-trillions-ai-race-beijing\n- Analogue: in the 2015–16 Chinese equity sell-off, Chinese outbound spending slowed but did not collapse, which argues for a modest rather than severe haircut (World Bank China Economic Update, 2016).\n- Dubai DET visitor statistics identify China as a top-ten source market, so DLD fees and tourism fees are exposed to Chinese wealth effects.	claude-opus-5-5	2026-10-29 19:30:49.223
