826bf78a-b52f-4ce9-aecb-7773e9310d8b	sig_mn_6d47b863f1	promote	Fed policy passes directly to UAE rates via the peg; the impact is material per 25bp but its sign is pending confirmation of the decision.	\N	Under the AED/USD peg, the CBUAE tracks Fed policy moves almost one-for-one. Any change transmits directly to Dubai through two channels: USFED (interest cost on floating-rate and refinanced government debt, plus yield on fiscal buffers) and RETXN (mortgage affordability, which drives DLD transaction fees). The input does not state the decision, so no signed point estimate is given. As an illustrative sensitivity, each 25bp move shifts net debt service by roughly AED ±0.1–0.15bn a year, assuming about AED 40–60bn of floating or refinancing exposure. Second-round effects on RETXN fees are likely smaller and lagged. Re-score once the rate decision is confirmed.	- Confirm the target-range decision and the CBUAE Base Rate response today, then re-run the 2027 debt-service projection.\n- If rates are cut: front-load planned 2027 refinancing and lock in fixed-rate sukuk tenors of 7–10 years while spreads are tight.\n- If rates are held or raised: lift the swap-hedged share of floating-rate debt towards 60–70% using AED/USD IRS.\n- Run a stress test on DLD fee revenue assuming a ±50bp mortgage-rate move, and adjust the 2027 RETXN budget line if needed.	- FOMC statement, 16 Sep 2026: https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260916a.htm\n- AED pegged at 3.6725/USD since 1997. CBUAE Base Rate is anchored to the Fed's IORB, so changes pass through to the UAE immediately.\n- 2022–23 analogue: CBUAE raised its base rate from 0.15% to 5.40% in step with the Fed, raising GRE and government funding costs (IMF UAE Article IV 2023). Dubai real-estate volumes nonetheless hit records, so the RETXN sensitivity is muted relative to the debt channel.	claude-opus-5-5	2026-10-29 19:33:26.368
