b2b1fdfa-5447-40bd-b7bb-6925b9a74a58	sig_mn_c4e03ba104	promote	Strong US growth shifts the Fed path, which transmits one-for-one through the AED peg to Dubai's cost of debt.	-0.15	US Q3 real GDP accelerated to 3.4% annualised from 2.2% in Q2. That reduces the case for Fed easing and supports higher-for-longer US Treasury yields and a firmer dollar. Because the AED is pegged to the USD, the Fed path passes directly into EIBOR and into the cost of Dubai's floating-rate and 2027 refinancing debt. Assuming roughly AED 60bn of repricing exposure and a +25bp shift in the expected rate path, the cost is about AED 0.15bn. A stronger DXY also mildly erodes tourism price-competitiveness against EUR- and INR-based visitors, partly offset by firmer US demand.	- Pre-fund part of the 2027 refinancing needs now, or lock in fixed rates via interest-rate swaps on around 30–40% of floating-rate exposure before any further repricing of UST10 and EIBOR.\n- Raise the 2027 budget interest-cost assumption by 25bp and stress-test a +50bp scenario.\n- Lengthen debt maturity at the next sukuk or bond issuance to reduce rollover sensitivity.\n- Keep fiscal buffers invested at the short end to capture higher yields on cash balances, which partly offsets debt costs.	- BEA advance estimate, Q3 2026 real GDP +3.4% annualised versus +2.2% in Q2: https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/gdp-advance-estimate-3rd-quarter-2026\n- BEA personal income +0.2% m/m in September, consistent with resilient demand: https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-september-2026\n- 2022–23 Fed hiking cycle: EIBOR tracked Fed funds almost one-for-one under the peg, lifting GCC sovereign and GRE borrowing costs (IMF Regional Economic Outlook, Middle East and Central Asia, 2023).	claude-opus-5-5	2026-10-29 20:09:39.569
