1e77afca-5d9b-4ecb-aa1e-ad9134067ccb	sig_mn_cefaf45a2f	promote	The Fed's projected path drives Dubai's cost of debt and buffer returns directly through the AED peg, so even small rate deviations reach the AED 0.1bn threshold.	-0.1	The AED is pegged to the USD, so EIBOR and Dubai's sukuk and loan pricing track the Fed path that the dot plot sets out for 2026–27. The input gives only the headline, not the projections themselves. The figure is therefore a sensitivity, not a forecast: roughly −AED 0.1bn of extra 2027 interest cost for each 25bp the median 2027 policy rate sits above the budget assumption. This assumes about AED 40–50bn of floating or refinancing exposure. Higher rates also raise income on fiscal buffers, which partly offsets the cost, and they dampen RETXN fee momentum. If the dot plot shows easing, the sign reverses.	- Within one week, read the June SEP median 2027 rate against the budget's rate assumption and re-base this estimate.\n- If the path is higher than budgeted, swap 30–50% of floating-rate government and GRE-guaranteed exposure to fixed at prevailing USD SOFR swap levels.\n- Bring forward 2027 refinancing (sukuk or loans) if UST10 is below the budget-case yield, and keep a 12-month maturity wall pre-funding cushion.\n- Re-test the RETXN fee forecast with mortgage rates 50bp higher, given the fee base's sensitivity to financing costs.	- Fed SEP release, June 16–17 2026: https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260617b.htm\n- FOMC statement, 17 June 2026: https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260617a.htm\n- CBUAE generally mirrors Fed policy-rate moves under the USD peg, so EIBOR transmits US rates directly.\n- Analogue: during the 2022–23 Fed tightening cycle, EIBOR rose sharply in step, raising UAE public and GRE refinancing costs.	claude-opus-5-5	2026-10-29 20:22:58.045
