f7e5b07d-880a-45be-839a-91d19b4dc46f	sig_mn_5c7def81b2	promote	Hormuz disruption combined with an EIA demand downgrade caps the oil upside and threatens Dubai's trade and tourism revenue base.	-1	EIA's June STEO cuts 2026 global oil demand because high prices, scarce fuel and government curbs are suppressing consumption, especially in Asia. Weaker demand caps the Brent upside from Hormuz disruption, so Dubai's modest hydrocarbon receipts get little windfall (BRENT, ~-0.4bn). Meanwhile the disruption and the Asian demand slowdown weigh on Jebel Ali throughput, re-exports and long-haul transit traffic. That hits customs duty (DXY/trade, ~-0.3bn) and tourism-linked fees and municipality charges (TOUR, ~-0.3bn) in 2027.	- Set the 2027 budget Brent assumption conservatively (e.g. $65/bbl) and treat any price spike as non-recurrent, not baseline revenue.\n- Hedge ~40% of 2027 oil-linked receipts via Brent puts/collars while Hormuz-driven volatility keeps option premia informative.\n- Route trade around Hormuz: prioritise Fujairah/Khor Fakkan port links and Etihad Rail capacity to protect Jebel Ali re-export flows and customs receipts.\n- Pre-approve a contingent tourism/aviation support package (fee deferrals, DXB incentive scheme) triggered if transit passengers fall >10% year on year.\n- Lift liquidity buffers: pre-fund 2027 refinancing needs now to avoid wider risk premia if Gulf shipping disruption persists.	- EIA June 2026 Short-Term Energy Outlook press release: /pressroom/releases/press589.php (lower 2026 global demand; price increases from Hormuz disruptions limited).\n- 2019 tanker attacks off Fujairah: Gulf war-risk insurance premia rose sharply and shipping costs increased (Reuters reporting, May–June 2019). This is an analogue for the trade-cost channel.\n- Dubai's revenue base is predominantly non-oil, with hydrocarbons a small share (Dubai DOF budget documents; IMF UAE Article IV). Trade and tourism channels therefore dominate the oil channel.	claude-opus-5-5	2026-10-29 20:23:39.092
