94b7114b-1f04-4077-a60d-bef289f01514	sig_mn_58b08f9c16	promote	Elevated Brent from persistent disruption produces a modest but material net oil-revenue gain, partly offset by Hormuz volume constraints.	0.4	EIA's May STEO assumes continued Middle East disruption, which keeps Brent elevated and raises the per-barrel value of Dubai's modest hydrocarbon receipts (BRENT channel). The gain is capped because Dubai's own offshore output exports from inside the Gulf and is volume-constrained by Hormuz. Most of the upside therefore comes from price on residual volumes and from ENOC/Dragon Oil upstream assets outside the Gulf. Oil is a single-digit share of Dubai revenue, so the net 2027 gain is small and uncertain.	- Hedge 30–40% of expected 2027 oil-linked receipts with Brent put spreads or collars near current forwards to lock in the windfall before any de-escalation.\n- Budget 2027 oil revenue at a conservative $70/bbl and ring-fence any excess into a stabilisation reserve, not recurrent spending.\n- Request updated ENOC/Dragon Oil dividend guidance reflecting upstream and refining-margin gains.\n- Model a zero-volume scenario for Dubai-origin crude while Hormuz is impaired.	- EIA May STEO press release: /pressroom/releases/press588.php (relative path as supplied)\n- World Bank, *Middle East War to Spark Biggest Energy Price Surge in Four Years*: https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMirgFBVV95cUxQcXZYeGQyQUkxX2hIR2VGTEVDSngzZE1NR2ljaHJSWjFGdTZjUWxxOHhIeHJmWlF6RkdwcnR2RjJnRWxkdnNUWW5CUV9qMnJnY3dsRXFCa3B0VjlVdlhDU01XVnFhbzYtcjdTcy1jNzljT2o3OUlrOUZwVllYdmdEMTlNTzVpdXhQbWtlTWZjX3FoTTM4eGtERGZLbmRJRkZ1dWY5SnhMNGhmRnlFQXc?oc=5\n- Analogue: in 2020 Brent averaged ~$42/bbl (EIA), yet Dubai's budget was far less exposed than oil-heavy GCC peers given its low oil share (DOF budget statements).	claude-opus-5-5	2026-10-29 20:25:02.294
