6e2f716e-5a6b-4e4d-bcc3-c4b9f42edee2	sig_mn_9a3b39d776	promote	The World Bank's latest outlook points to a lower oil-price path through 2026, a direct risk to Dubai's oil revenue and an indirect drag on non-oil activity of roughly AED 0.5bn.	-0.5	The October 2025 Commodity Markets Outlook extends the World Bank's softer energy-price path into 2026, driven by an oil supply surplus as OPEC+ unwinds cuts and global demand growth slows. BRENT channel: Dubai's direct hydrocarbon receipts are a modest share of revenue (indicatively ~5%, i.e. a few AED bn), so a sustained ~USD 8–10/bbl shortfall versus budget assumptions implies roughly AED 0.3–0.4bn of lost oil revenue in 2027. A smaller indirect drag comes through NOGDP and PMI, as weaker GCC liquidity dampens regional trade, property demand and fee-generating activity. Combined central estimate: about AED -0.5bn. The feed does not include the report's figures, so this rests on directional channels and the price levels should be checked against the source.	- Re-base the 2027 budget oil-price assumption to the World Bank or futures-curve path (whichever is lower) in the next budget cycle, and stress-test at USD 55/bbl.\n- Hedge 30–40% of expected 2026–27 oil-linked receipts via Brent swaps or put spreads while forwards remain in the mid-USD 60s.\n- Ring-fence capex phasing: identify AED 1–2bn of non-critical 2027 projects that can be deferred if Brent averages below USD 60.\n- Accelerate non-oil revenue diversification (fee indexation, corporate tax collection efficiency) to offset the oil-revenue drag.	- World Bank, *Commodity Markets Outlook — October 2025*: https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMilwFBVV95cUxOT2owVzNFa212dzBNemlkVVlvZnJMcDhBZS0wNWxpVkFNV2dhY25kYUQ4SDJXQ1hGU2lzeHlrazVxQlBEbWdGYk1hME1vRlAxV3k0dHFQSUljVG5yZ3hDYTlvNnJPMEFsaHdoYXNRaHNsTzRwakR4dExlZGtVUXRiajNnSlJBVXdTOThkMkhFOEc1dnBVbjFZ?oc=5\n- Earlier signal of the same downtrend: World Bank, *Falling Commodity Prices Could Mute Inflation Risks* (Apr 2025).\n- Historical analogue: the 2015–16 oil downturn led Dubai and other UAE emirates to consolidate spending, with non-oil growth slowing (IMF UAE Article IV consultations, 2016–17).	claude-opus-5-5	2026-10-29 20:27:45.491
