587233b1-7408-4b8d-ae8d-5ba1b1dc1f09	sig_mn_50ba427056	promote	A World Bank outlook of a structural oil surplus capping Brent in the low $70s materially lowers Dubai's oil-linked revenue outlook for 2026–27.	-0.4	The World Bank expects a global oil supply surplus, driven by soft Chinese demand, non-OPEC+ output growth and spare OPEC+ capacity, to cap Brent near the low-$70s in 2025–26 even if the Middle East conflict widens. Its October 2024 Commodity Markets Outlook projects Brent at about $73/bbl in 2025 and $72/bbl in 2026, versus roughly $80 in 2024. On Dubai's oil-linked receipts, which are a single-digit share of total revenue, a ~10% lower price implies a loss of roughly AED 0.3–0.5bn in 2027. Weaker GCC oil liquidity also creates modest second-round risk to RETXN and PMI-linked fees, partly offset by lower fuel costs for aviation and tourism.	- Set the 2027 budget oil-price assumption conservatively at $65/bbl Brent, with a $55/bbl stress scenario in the medium-term fiscal framework.\n- Evaluate a zero-cost collar on about 30–40% of expected 2026–27 oil-linked revenue, with a floor near $65/bbl, while forward curves remain in the low $70s.\n- Ring-fence any 2025 oil windfall above budget into fiscal buffers rather than recurrent spending.\n- Accelerate non-oil revenue measures (fee indexation, corporate-tax compliance) to offset the oil shortfall.\n- Monitor the pace of the OPEC+ voluntary-cut unwind monthly and re-run revenue projections after each OPEC+ ministerial meeting.	- World Bank, *Oversupply Could Mute Effects of Wider Middle-East Conflict on Oil Prices* (Oct 2024): https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMisAFBVV95cUxNTHFrVk9OOFFnZGY3UzAzQVpDRWppNjNGeTBzbDdNT053Q3lnWENYNXdXeGNQNzI0d0FUUHV1c3IwRDRKTFFqVnFIVWFTVWJwSkpxRTBFUnhsZjZsVVFaNnItSjhNQXFPdkZpc2htSl9kYXRIRFFzRGpYWDMtWkJySnpVY0F1OHFod2xraXNwcDdxZVc5a0g5QjQ3QWFCb1d0LVdMR0toMkFjalBuZC13OQ?oc=5\n- World Bank, *Commodity Markets Outlook, October 2024*, projects Brent at about $73/bbl (2025) and $72/bbl (2026): https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMilwFBVV95cUxOWnNRMjVPVUpfLThMcklEckhSTlpGbEk1cUN0TE1yWFFlOG9ERm16MjlMeE96TVBfRGhvRzREM2RVOG9NdDB4M1B5R09RelFZVEswVGY5bDdpT1A0bl9PaTZ2aE1BRUM4eHVBbzMxS2NYVEs1a3NFQ2hlUjl3cEo5R29QaFRNU3F2QS1iTk9kbTlfQ1BmcVlB?oc=5\n- Historical analogue: Brent averaged about $64/bbl in 2019 and about $42/bbl in 2020 (−35%), which compressed GCC oil receipts (EIA; IMF regional outlooks).	claude-opus-5-5	2026-10-29 20:30:50.571
