f6716bc4-e9ce-46af-a285-14e85053a70d	sig_mn_be48f1c23e	promote	Iran-US escalation plus an oil spike creates offsetting but net-negative multi-channel exposure for Dubai at or above the AED 0.1bn threshold.	-0.2	SBP's hike to 12% signals escalating Iran-US tensions and rising oil prices. Higher Brent lifts Dubai's small oil revenue line (BRENT, about +AED 0.2bn), but a Gulf risk premium hits tourism receipts (TOUR), Iran-linked re-exports and customs duty (DXY/trade), and raises sovereign and GRE refinancing spreads (USFED). Pakistani monetary tightening also dampens remittance-funded property demand from a top buyer nationality (RETXN). Net effect: about -AED 0.2bn, low confidence on a headline-only basis.	- Run an immediate Hormuz-disruption stress test on 2027 revenue at Brent $95 and $110, with tourism down 10%.\n- Lock in fixed-rate refinancing for 2027 maturities now, before Gulf CDS spreads widen.\n- Bank any oil windfall above the budget Brent assumption into the fiscal reserve rather than spending it.\n- Have DET pre-position risk-reassurance marketing in Europe and India.\n- Monitor Iran-linked re-export volumes weekly via Dubai Customs.	- Source: [Pakistan Times](https://www.pakistantimes.com/sbp-raises-interest-rate-to-12-amid-iran-us-tensions-and-rising-oil-prices-15846/)\n- Related: [Express Tribune](https://tribune.com.pk/story/2637107/sbp-raises-policy-rate-to-12)\n- Analogue: the 2019 tanker attacks and Abqaiq strike lifted Brent about 15% intraday and widened Gulf risk premia (EIA, Reuters).\n- Analogue: the 2012 Iran sanctions cut Dubai-Iran trade sharply (IMF UAE Article IV 2013).	claude-opus-5-5	2026-10-29 20:32:57.578
