6e0b72a4-1edc-4775-8b90-6940985cca25	sig_mn_d8d609837f	promote	A ~$115 crude shock with a sharply weaker rupee materially moves both Dubai's oil royalties (gain) and Indian tourism and property demand (loss).	1.3	Brent at ~$115 sits far above a typical ~$65–70 budget assumption, which lifts Dubai's oil royalties and ENOC- and Dubai Petroleum-linked receipts (BRENT). Even if 2027 averages only ~$90–100, this indicatively adds ~AED 1.6–1.9bn. Rupee weakness (INR 95/USD) and higher Indian fuel inflation squeeze outbound travel and property buying by Dubai's largest source market. That cuts tourism fees, Tourism Dirham and hotel levies by ~AED 0.3–0.5bn (TOUR, RETXN). Higher fuel costs also raise outlays at RTA and other GREs, giving a net gain of ~AED 1.3bn, with low confidence.	- Ring-fence the windfall: book any oil receipts above the budget price into reserves or early debt amortisation, not recurrent spending.\n- Lock in part of the gain: hedge ~30–40% of 2027 oil-linked revenue via Brent swaps or collars near $90/bbl, as forward curves are likely backwardated.\n- Protect the India market: work with DET on INR-priced packages, UPI/RuPay acceptance and targeted visa and airline promotions.\n- Re-price fuel-sensitive expenditure lines (RTA fleet, utilities) in the 2027 budget and review GRE dividend assumptions for airline fuel costs.	- Rupee at 95.19, crude at $115 — DNA India: https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiqgFBVV95cUxONUtBZ3B3alBGWUV2Y040XzBENk5SMjM4bk5IaDkzNlBwSWVxM2psSlhNT1FGU3FXdXU5NENjYTNFVjRqOXByRkFzVTFGTm5JQ2dIanlmZktCMHRibi1JYmRHZVJuOE02VUI0MzVrZV9wemlSaVg1MXRFczFyZlIzdVk2c0NVM2EzdW9ydS1kWkppdVZqcy00bTJFT19GMVByd3FhOTU0WjBxdw?oc=5\n- 2022 analogue: Brent averaged ~$99 (EIA) and GCC fiscal balances swung to surplus (IMF Regional Economic Outlook, MENA, 2023).\n- India is Dubai's largest source market for international visitors (Dubai DET visitor statistics).	claude-opus-5-5	2026-10-30 20:58:20.203
